WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.230
Maybe there's something about the role of
Martin Riggs that leads to trouble.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:10.170
Originally introduced in the 1987 buddy cop
movie Lethal Weapon, the Green Beret-turned-emotionally-unbalanced

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.799
detective was made famous by Mel Gibson, playing
opposite Danny Glover as the straight-laced

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:15.830
Roger Murtaugh.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.680
The movie was a crowd-pleasing hit that spawned
three sequels through the 80s and 90s, eventually

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.760
finding its way to rebirth as a TV show on
Fox in 2016.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.760
Clayne Crawford and Damon Wayans stepped into
the roles of Riggs and Murtaugh for the TV

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.580
series, bringing a chemistry that critics
praised.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.370
But behind the scenes, things weren't quite
so rosy.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.360
After weeks of rumored on-set tensions, Fox
announced that Crawford would not be returning

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.270
for Lethal Weapon's third season, replacing
him instead with Seann William Scott.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.129
It's a remarkable move for an established
series to make - here are the real reasons

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:48.110
why it happened.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.989
Anger management

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:54.300
News of Lethal Weapon's uncertain future first
broke in late April when Deadline reported

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.949
that the well-performing series was in danger
of cancellation, allegedly due to Clayne Crawford's

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:00.120
on-set behavior.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.140
According to the report, Crawford had gained
a reputation of emotional abuse against fellow

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.780
cast and crew, contributing to a hostile work
environment that threatened to single-handedly

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.970
sink the series.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.330
The report also said that Crawford had been
disciplined several times for his behavior.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:17.510
Crawford addressed the report in a statement
on Instagram, confirming that he had been

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.540
reprimanded twice during production.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.540
According to the actor,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.420
"I reacted with anger over working conditions
that did not feel safe or conducive to good

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.810
work".

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.180
On-set safety

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.360
The drama came to a head after the announcement
of Crawford's firing, which occurred just

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.580
before the second season's final episode.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.230
In response to questions and outcry from disappointed
fans, Damon Wayans took to Twitter to shed

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.420
some light on the on-set issues.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.770
Wayans had set his account to private in the
wake of the firing, then unlocked it to advance

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.610
the case against his former co-star.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.650
Afterwards, he deleted his Twitter presence
entirely.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.060
Wayans' primary piece of evidence against
Crawford came in the form of photos and a

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.930
video taken during production of an episode
that Crawford not only starred in, but directed.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.360
It seems that during the filming of a shootout
action sequence, Wayans was struck in the

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.610
back of the head by shrapnel from an effect
gone awry.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:10.670
Tagging his tweets with phrases like "#directedby
@claynecrawford" and "#noapology," Wayans

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.640
made it clear that he blamed his co-star for
an inadequate level of safety when placed

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.640
in charge.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:24.800
"See now, what we have here is a failure to
communicate."

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.710
Spilled tea

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:31.090
Wayans went on to reveal an alleged history
of misdeeds by Crawford and the anxiety that

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.600
the rest of the cast and crew felt in his
presence.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.021
Citing a "file of infractions" and appealing
to Warner Bros. Entertainment for help, Wayans

00:02:38.021 --> 00:02:41.420
broadened his accusations, stating, "…it
wasn't just me."

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.840
He then shared a photo of a sticker that he
claimed other people had placed around the

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.490
Warner Bros. lot, emblazoned with the words
"Clayne Crawford is an emotional terrorist."

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.950
Another specific offense cited by Wayans was
a claim that Crawford "hit another actor in

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.380
the mouth with a bottle of green tea and busted
his mouth open."

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.070
Wayans tagged the accusation "#notanaccident."

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.140
Riggs is ready to go

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.160
Just as in the original film, the TV series'
Martin Riggs enters the story while grieving

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.580
over the loss of his wife.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.020
And, just as in Lethal Weapon 2, he eventually
discovers that a drug lord is responsible

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.250
for her death and sets out for vengeance.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.100
The second season found him bringing the killer
to justice, bringing closure to the character's

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.000
primary arc, if not all of his neuroses.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.540
As Crawford explained in an interview with
Assignment X, "We can't play on the death

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.750
for four seasons".

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.319
Though he went on to explain that the show
would be "planting little seeds" as to the

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:34.080
character's future, the season finale ended
with a cliffhanger that had him shot in the

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.819
chest by his half-brother.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:39.770
With Riggs' revenge plot wrapped up and his
life hanging in the balance, writing Crawford

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:44.910
out of the show likely doesn't present Lethal
Weapon's writers with much of a challenge.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:45.910
Moving on

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.690
Perhaps mindful of his growing reputation
for tension, Crawford put on a pleasant, if

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.770
slightly snarky, front in the wake of his
firing.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.440
As news first broke that Fox was searching
for a replacement, he took to Twitter to share

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.970
a playfully shrugging selfie captioned simply,
"Hmmmmmm?"

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.940
He followed this with a separate tweet reading,
"Wait, wait, wait……you can't fire me on

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:05.940
my day off!"

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.180
A more solemn Instagram post followed, in
which Crawford expressed sincere thanks to

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.920
the fans and congratulations to the cast and
crew on the show's renewal.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.850
Whatever happens next for Crawford, it seems
possible that Lethal Weapon's days of drama

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.970
at least the kind behind the camera may well
be over.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:21.970
Thanks for watching!

